---
// /hosting/leave/: the exit path. The repository is already in the researcher's own GitHub account
// (D00-2): leaving the registry moves nothing. The page says how to unlink it or ask for the layer's
// deletion (the repository's settings, E8), how to keep a complete backup with git alone (clone
// --mirror, a bundle), how to get GitHub's migration archive of the repository's metadata, how to
// save the registry's layer for the repository as JSON (read from the static shard and saved by the
// browser: src/scripts/import-repo.ts), where a citable copy goes (Zenodo, figshare), and how to
// restore a history from Software Heritage.
//
// A static page: its form is shown by its script, whose only request is the static shard
// /forge/layer/NN.json (0 Worker requests). Every command comes from src/lib/import-commands.ts, with
// placeholders in capitals. science.css only; the platform's name from src/config.ts.
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../../config";
import { backupCommands, commandText, migrationArchiveCommands, swhRestoreCommands } from "../../lib/import-commands";
import { DATA_HOMES } from "../../lib/limits-text";
import { GRACE_SECONDS } from "../../../worker/forge/service/caps.ts";

const GRACE_DAYS = Math.round(GRACE_SECONDS / 86_400);

const REPO = { owner: "OWNER", name: "NAME" };
const backup = commandText(backupCommands(REPO));
const backupLfs = commandText(backupCommands(REPO, { lfs: true }));
const migration = commandText(migrationArchiveCommands(REPO));
const SWHID = `swh:1:rev:${"0".repeat(40)}`;
const restore = commandText(swhRestoreCommands(SWHID, { owner: "OWNER", name: "NAME-restored" }));
const LINKS = {
  migrationsApi: "https://docs.github.com/en/rest/migrations/users",
  cli: "https://cli.github.com/",
  bundle: "https://git-scm.com/docs/git-bundle",
  figshare: "https://help.figshare.com/article/how-to-connect-figshare-with-your-github-account",
  swh: "https://archive.softwareheritage.org/",
  swhVault: "https://docs.softwareheritage.org/devel/swh-vault/",
};
---
<Base
  title="Leaving the registry"
  description="Your repository stays yours: unlink it, back it up, take the registry's layer with you."
  breadcrumb={[{ text: "Hosting code", href: "/hosting/" }, { text: "Leaving the registry" }]}
>
  <h1>Leaving the registry</h1>
  <p class="summary">
    Your repository is already in your own GitHub account: leaving {SITE_NAME} moves nothing and deletes nothing on
    GitHub. What {SITE_NAME} keeps is a layer around it (the papers it is linked to, tracing maps, script copies under
    licences that allow it), and you can take that with you too.
  </p>

  <h2 id="unlink">Unlink it, or ask for its layer's deletion</h2>
  <p>
    From the repository's page on {SITE_NAME}, under Settings: unlink it from its papers (one authorized action, as
    you, on GitHub), or ask for its layer to be deleted. The layer then leaves the site after a grace period of {GRACE_DAYS}{" "}
    days during which you can change your mind. Deleting the repository on GitHub itself stays your own act, on GitHub or
    from the same page, with a fresh authorization: {SITE_NAME} never does it for you. Script copies and tracing maps
    of a paper are removed with a removal request, from the paper's own page.
  </p>

  <form id="leave-form" hidden>
    <p>
      <label for="leave-repo">Your repository</label> (account/name, or its GitHub address)<br />
      <input type="text" id="leave-repo" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" maxlength="200" />
    </p>
    <p><label><input type="checkbox" id="leave-lfs" /> It uses Git LFS: back up its large files too</label></p>
    <p><button type="submit">Show the commands for it</button></p>
    <p id="leave-message" role="status"></p>
  </form>
  <section id="leave-result" class="setup" hidden>
    <h3>Its settings on {SITE_NAME}</h3>
    <p id="leave-settings"></p>
    <h3>A complete backup with git</h3>
    <pre class="commands" id="leave-backup" data-copy="the backup commands"></pre>
    <h3>GitHub's migration archive of its metadata</h3>
    <pre class="commands" id="leave-migration" data-copy="the migration archive commands"></pre>
    <h3>The registry's layer, as JSON</h3>
    <p>
      Read from the site's public files and saved by your browser: the papers it is linked to and what the registry
      shows about it.
    </p>
    <p><button type="button" id="leave-export">Prepare the file</button></p>
    <p id="leave-export-message" role="status"></p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="backup">A complete backup with git</h2>
  <p>
    A mirror clone holds every branch, tag and commit; a <a href={LINKS.bundle}>bundle</a> puts all of it in one file you
    can keep anywhere and clone from later. OWNER is your account, NAME the repository:
  </p>
  <pre class="commands" data-copy="the backup commands">{backup}</pre>
  <p>With Git LFS, its large files are fetched into the mirror too (they are not in the bundle: keep the folder):</p>
  <pre class="commands" data-copy="the backup commands with LFS">{backupLfs}</pre>

  <h2 id="metadata">Issues, pull requests and the rest: GitHub's migration archive</h2>
  <p>
    Git keeps the code and its history; the issues, pull requests, comments, releases and wiki are GitHub's metadata.
    GitHub exports them as a migration archive (<a href={LINKS.migrationsApi}>the user migrations API</a>), here with
    <a href={LINKS.cli}>GitHub's command-line tool</a> and your own sign-in. The repository is not locked while it is
    prepared. Options leave out the Git data, the metadata, attachments, releases or projects when you do not need
    them (exclude_… set to true).
  </p>
  <pre class="commands" data-copy="the migration archive commands">{migration}</pre>

  <h2 id="layer">The registry's layer</h2>
  <p>
    Everything {SITE_NAME} shows publicly about a repository comes from its public files: the form above saves it as
    one JSON file, made in your browser. Tracing maps validated by an author have their own DOI on Zenodo, and stay
    citable wherever the code goes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="citation">A citable copy</h2>
  <p>
    A DOI for a release of the code itself comes from an archive, not from a host:
    <a href={DATA_HOMES.zenodoGithub}>Zenodo's GitHub integration</a> or
    <a href={LINKS.figshare}>figshare's</a> archive each release you publish on GitHub, with its own DOI.
  </p>

  <h2 id="software-heritage">Restoring from Software Heritage</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={LINKS.swh}>Software Heritage</a> keeps public code for the long term; {SITE_NAME} asks it to save a
    repository when a person asks. A history it holds can be restored into an empty repository through its
    <a href={LINKS.swhVault}>vault</a>: replace the identifier below (swh:1:rev:…) by the one Software Heritage shows for
    the commit, and NAME-restored by the empty repository you created.
  </p>
  <pre class="commands" data-copy="the restore commands">{restore}</pre>

  <h2 id="more">More</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/repositories/">Your repositories</a>, and their settings.</li>
    <li><a href="/hosting/import/">Importing a repository</a>, the other way round.</li>
    <li><a href="/hosting/">The hosting guides</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <script>
    import "../../scripts/import-repo";
  </script>
</Base>
